2026-10-17  Petter Sjölund <ignalina@me.com>

	* Unreleased; changes since Spatterlight 1.5.4.

	* Map: pan with the arrow buttons and zoom with the + and - buttons in
	the pane's corner, or "glk zoom N" (3 to 32; below 10 the rooms lose
	their names).  The buttons also work while the game waits for a key or
	a pop-up answer, the map is redrawn at each such pause, rooms on
	another level are drawn fainter, the Up and Down badges show arrows,
	and the default palette gained in-between tints.  Clicking just off a
	side or corner of the current room walks one step through that exit,
	even into an unseen room; a map-click walk starts on the prompt's own
	line and no longer runs half-typed input or draws an "I don't
	understand" in ADRIFT 4 games.  Removed "glk map colour".
	* New "glk notify" sets score notification and "glk statusline" (or
	"glk stats") prints the status line, for when a game's own task or a
	build without SCARE's meta-commands puts the plain words out of reach.
	"glk summary" no longer turns verbose on as it reports it, and an
	abbreviation such as "glk stat" or "glk col" that also prefixes the
	command's aliases is no longer ambiguous.
	* Text set in Wingdings, Wingdings 3 or Symbol is translated to
	Unicode, as Webdings already was.
	* Autosave at a "press a key" pause, at the ADRIFT 4 name and gender
	prompts, and at ADRIFT 5 pop-up questions and pending "Get what?"
	questions.  The ADRIFT 4 autosave also keeps the AGAIN buffer, the
	command history, the pronouns, a pending "Which one?" and the verbose
	and notify settings.  A Spatterlight autosave regression suite (make
	autosave) covers both engines.
	* Media a game names but never packed into its file is looked up beside
	the .taf by file name, whatever its case.  Fixed an occasional crash
	loading a picture or sound after an autorestore; the Blorb resource map
	is registered again after one.
	* ADRIFT 3.7 to 4: 37 games whose own data makes them unwinnable are
	patched as they load, each keyed to the exact release ("glk patches
	off" disables).  A title picture is a title screen of its own or sits
	above the first text.  The assists share one "glk assist" command, with
	"glk assist repeat" and "glk assist room" new, and games known to need
	one get it turned on automatically with a notice at startup.
	* ADRIFT 3.7 and 3.8 games follow their own Runners rather than 3.9's:
	the wording for take, put, open and refusals, PUT IN and PUT ON as one
	handler, container listings, the disambiguation prompt, and the
	descriptions and restrictions those versions evaluate differently.
	Where Runner parity would only have made the parser understand less,
	Scarier keeps UNDO, Z, LOOK X, comma splitting, GO TO and the other
	verbs, and retries an unmatched command under looser rules; the text
	stays grammatical where a Runner printed an accident.
	* A few more deliberate departures from the Runners: LOOK AROUND shows
	the room at every version, GET OFF X answers "You are not standing on
	anything!" instead of taking X, and before 4.0 a task that runs and
	prints nothing says "Done." rather than the game's "don't understand"
	text, and no longer hides the object's own text on a READ line.
	* Random numbers come from a stream compatible with the original
	Runners (SCR_RNG=xoshiro), so chance-driven games and the walkthroughs
	that depend on them play out as they do there.
	* Battle system settled further against the 3.9 and 4.0 Runners: a blow
	strikes every character the line names, the "Who do you want to
	attack?" and "with what?" questions carry into the next line, THROW is
	a battle verb, stamina recovery revives a fallen character, the
	narration follows the game's perspective, and attribute changes land as
	both Runners apply them.
	* The meta-commands are the Runner's own: PAST, BYE, END and ENDGAME
	are understood, and a dozen invented commands no longer take a line
	away from the game's tasks.  SCARE's own words (VERBOSE, NOTIFY,
	HINTS, STATUSLINE, STATS and the rest) stand aside whenever the game
	has a task whose command matches the line.  UNDO prints the restored turn's output in
	each version's wording.
	* Another long round of parser and world-model work measured command by
	command against all four Runners: noun resolution and its tie-breaks,
	the "Which one?" prompt, PUT and TAKE precedence and the auto-supplied
	"from", capacity and the carried-load model, what counts as seen, ALR
	and synonym rewriting, wildcard task matching, the line splitter, and
	the rule of one game task per typed line.  Bold, italics and underline
	are on/off toggles as in the Runners, and an intro's unclosed style no
	longer reaches the first room.  Walk steps land on the exact tick, a
	stopped walk still stamps the walker, events recheck a run task's
	restrictions, and the turn an event starts is itself a tick.
	* A Cyrillic or accented capital in a battle line spelling the
	player's name, or in a refused command, is now folded through the
	game's code page like the rest of the line, instead of being left
	alone and failing to match.
	* A game or saved game that names an object, character, room, task
	or event that does not exist, in a place the loader does not already
	repair, now stops the game cleanly instead of aborting the
	interpreter, and says why: under Spatterlight in an error sheet,
	elsewhere in the main window.
	* Performance: the ALR table is indexed instead of scanned per line,
	undo snapshots are no longer compressed, the map's unchanging data is
	read once per game rather than at every prompt, the map is flushed as
	merged rectangles rather than one-row spans, and an ADRIFT 5 game no
	longer takes a full state snapshot every turn when autosave is off and
	reuses its unchanged sections when it does.  Command matching is
	cheaper, the ALR lookup is filtered before it searches, and ADRIFT 5
	property and group lookups are hashed.
	The one-second pause before a finished game restarts honours the
	delays preference.
	* ADRIFT 5: identical <# #> tags in one block of text all show the
	first one's value, as the Runner's replace-all does; music turned off
	at an opening question stays off; a finished sound can be replayed.
	Builds with GCC, Visual C++ and clang-cl, and plays ADRIFT 5 pictures and sounds under
	Windows Glk.
	* Source layout: a top-level review split scrunner.cpp into runner/,
	sclibrar.cpp into topic fragments, os_glk into glk/, sctafpar.cpp into
	parser, fixups and patches, scrun_dispatch.cpp into tables, dispatch,
	input and census, and the a5run and a5text families into subfolders
	under adrift5/.  A second pass moved the ADRIFT 4 engine into core/
	with one prototype header per module in place of scprotos.h, the
	console and autosave files into platform/, the dump and trace tools
	into tools/ and the test stubs into headless/; split scparser,
	scprintf, sctasks, the disambiguation, take and put libraries by
	topic and some forty long functions into stages; gathered each
	module's file-scope state into one struct; opens the game file by
	path in one place; and lets the Makefile find its sources by
	wildcard.  A third pass split another dozen long functions into
	helpers and removed the last goto, and scarier.h is now documented as
	a supported embedding API for other hosts.  Every step verified
	against the headless suite's recorded output.
	* Test tree: test/adrift4 and test/adrift5, with sha256 manifests and
	fetch_games.sh for the corpora.  The ADRIFT 4 walkthrough suite grew to
	675 rows, backed by a library of transcripts recorded from the original
	3.7, 3.8, 3.9 and 4.0 Runners; a headless scmap_dump map renderer, now
	seeded like the other harnesses.  notes/ holds the comparisons with
	SCARE 1.3.10, FrankenDrift and between ADRIFT 4 and 5, and the
	ADRIFT 5 XML notes.

2026-08-17  Petter Sjölund <ignalina@me.com>

	* Released with Spatterlight 1.5.4.

	* Text colours are displayed, toggled with "glk colour".  Bold, italic
	and right-justified text in ADRIFT 4 games.
	* New "glk undo", "glk restore", "glk restart" and "glk quit" for when
	the standard commands are unavailable, as at the name prompt.  "glk map
	colour" switches the map to a scheme derived from the current theme,
	remembered per game.
	* The map draws its connectors as the Runner does, with opaque badges
	and arrowheads on one-way exits, and no longer opens an empty pane for
	a hidden player.  ADRIFT 5 maps open when the game asks, and draw In
	and Out connectors with a badge at both ends.
	* Plays the two known ADRIFT 3.7 games.  Reads and writes the pre-4.0
	save format as well as the 4.0 one, so saves move between Spatterlight
	and the old Runner in either direction.
	* Symbol fonts such as the dove under the Topaz title are translated to
	Unicode.  The right-hand status text no longer overwrites the room name
	in a narrow window.
	* Container capacity is a volume rather than a count, as in the Runner.
	Timed events and NPC walks match the Runners in detail: immediate
	events start while the game loads, zero-length and restarting events
	fire the right number of times, walk arrivals trigger their tasks once,
	and event room descriptions print last.
	* Battle system settled command by command against the 3.9 and 4.0
	Runners: weapons and armour, wielding, thrown weapons, the combat
	narration and the status display.
	* Fixed a random-number bug that made choices between two outcomes far
	less random than intended.
	* Parser and world-model fixes: PUT precedence, taking and dropping,
	restrictions that reach into closed carried containers, the "You pick
	up" / "You take" version split, the pre-4.0 refusals and death message,
	where the ending message appears, room-name headings, and LAST and
	PREVIOUS as AGAIN.  The Runners' end-of-game score summary is printed,
	and score notification is off by default.
	* ADRIFT 5: pop-up questions such as the player's name and gender are
	asked in the story window; an adventure with no locations is refused
	with the Runner's error; the <del> tag erases earlier text, even across
	turns.  A large batch of engine fixes found by the adrift-5-rs
	conformance suite, imported as test/a5probes/, and by reviewing the
	file format: restrictions, task actions, walks, user functions, object
	references, conversation and a dozen text functions.  ADRIFT-5-XML.md,
	a hand-written specification of the adventure and save formats,
	contributed by Dan Fabulich.
	* Harness: sha256 manifests and a fetch script for the game corpora;
	the ADRIFT 4 walkthrough suite grew from 26 to 242 rows.

2026-07-29  Petter Sjölund <ignalina@me.com>

	* Scarier 1.5.0, first released with Spatterlight 1.5.2.

	* Forked from SCARE 1.3.10 as C++, with the sc_ prefixes renamed scr_,
	and joined by an ADRIFT 5 engine derived from FrankenDrift under
	adrift5/, so that one interpreter plays every known ADRIFT game.
	* Map display, at the side or the top, zoomable with glk commands.
	Sound and graphics.  Autosave and autorestore.
	* ADRIFT 4: the 4.0 battle system (stamina, weapons and armour) and the
	very different 3.8/3.9 one.  "glk combatassist" and "glk moveassist"
	for games broken by their conversion to 4.0, "glk capacity" for the
	original carrying limits, and "glk verbose" for games whose own VERBOSE
	does not work.  Loads and saves the original 4.0 save format.  Asks for
	the player's name and gender at the start when the game did.  Events
	and NPC actions run in the Runner's order.  No spurious "You can't go
	in any direction!" after a room description; the Glk single-letter
	shortcuts no longer shadow single-letter game commands; crashes on
	malformed game data fixed; full support for LAIR of the CYBER-COW.
	* ADRIFT 5: every game found plays from start to finish, matching
	FrankenDrift.  Multi-level undo.  Saves in the Runner's and
	FrankenDrift's .tas format.  Danish and real-time events supported;
	font tags and text colours not yet.  The ADRIFT 4 glk meta-commands
	(transcript, inputlog, summary and so on) work here too.
	* Harness: a FrankenDrift ground-truth diff harness, and a walkthrough
	regression suite for both engines with a save/restore self-check.

2008-05-01  Simon Baldwin <simon_baldwin@yahoo.com>

	* Release 1.3.10.

	* Fixed a CR/LF problem that prevents a game that contains rogue
	newline or carriage return characters (ones that don't precede a
	newline) from loading.
	* Corrected a (probably rare to trigger) bug in TAF file decompression
	related to leaving the decompression loop before the zlib inflation
	output buffer had been completely consumed.
	* Amended status line handling for better results with game room
	names and status strings that are blank.
	* Corrected two bugs in the Cyrillic codepage 866 to gost 16876-71
	ascii conversion tables.
	* Removed use of labs() on sc_int types, to better decouple sc_int
	from intrinsic C long types.
	* Cleaned up action on zero-byte allocations, undefined in ANSI/ISO C.
	* Improved hints iterator functionality by splitting it into two
	separate functions, one for the first hint, and one for the next; this
	is a minor SCARE API change.
	* Yet more "adjustments" to tasks triggered by NPC and/or player
	movement.  These are still not right, but the change at least fixes
	breakage in The PK Girl introduced with release 1.3.9.
	* Minor code refactoring and formatting changes.

2008-03-12  Simon Baldwin <simon_baldwin@yahoo.com>

	* Release 1.3.9.

	* Fixed disambiguation problem with "go to the <location>".  If a
	location was both, say, "south" and "out", "go to the <location>" would
	previously be reported as ambiguous.
	* Ignore attempts to move player to "hidden".  Possible where a task
	moves the player to same location as an NPC and that NPC is hidden.
	Reported by ElliotM, with bug triage by Mark J. Tilford.
	* Eliminated some memcpy calls and memory shuffling, leading to a
	slight increase in performance.
	* Added a new SCARE-specific system variable, %scare_version%, that
	allows a game to query the version of SCARE.  This variable overrides
	any user-defined variable of the same name, provided that variable's
	value is zero.
	* Switched to a more efficient exponentiation implementation, based on
	the Russian Peasant algorithm.
	* Changes to NPC walks to try to better replicate Adrift's somewhat
	inscrutable actions in this area.
	* Fixed a couple of bugs found by gcc's Mudflap, including a rather
	nasty one involving object accessors called on building a gamestate.
	* Changed from platform ctype functions (isspace, toupper, etc) to
	specialized internal ones for codepage 1252 or codepage 1251, depending
	on the game locale.  Again, a slight performance gain here.
	* Added codepage 1252 to iso 8859-1 conversion to the Glk interface for
	status lines; until now, only main window text had been converted.
	* Improved codepage 1252 conversions for characters not supported by a
	Glk library, and added codepage 850 conversions for courier and
	terminal fonts.
	* Added Unicode output for Glk libraries that support it.  This should
	help to further hide the differences between Windows codepages and
	Glk output, especially codepage 850 output.
	* Added codepage 1251 and 866 Cyrillic codepage support to the Glk
	interface for Russian games, automatically selected if SCARE detects a
	Russian language game on starting the Glk interface.
	* Added cancellation of <wait x.x> tags in the Glk interface; pressing
	space or return cancels the delay.
	* Assorted code tidy up and style changes.

2007-09-14  Simon Baldwin <simon_baldwin@yahoo.com>

	* Release 1.3.8.

	* Fixed a bug with "Also here is the" message and plural objects.
	Thanks to Nick Rogers for the bug report.
	* Added error handling for unterminated quoted strings in expressions.
	* Implemented printfilter escaping, so that player input that is routed
	to %text% and then subsequently displayed does not trigger <> tags,
	& entities, or variable substitions.
	* Added static_unmoved to the field comparisons in the debugger.
	* Reworked room name and description code so that it correctly uses
	alternate description strings when required by game states.  Added a
	new testcase for these features.  Thanks to Sam Trenholme for the
	report and bug triage.
	* Assorted small code tidy up and style changes.

2007-06-26  Simon Baldwin <simon_baldwin@yahoo.com>

	* Release 1.3.7.

	* Corrected a printfilter bug that could let &gt; and other characters
	through into game ouput untranslated under certain circumstances.
	* Fixed a bug that could cause incorrect object descriptions to appear
	for objects moved by anything other than the player, and another that
	failed to ignore "stale" task reverse commands.  Thanks once more to
	Nick Rogers for the reports and test cases.
	* Added the ability to take objects from named NPCs to the parser.
	* Implemented "get off" as a way to move the player from on supporter
	object to on the ground.
	* Added "go to room_name" as a way to move to a named adjacent room.
	* Assorted relatively wide-ranging changes to standard system messages
	and a couple of new standard commands, to align more closely with the
	Runner.  No prizes for guessing who reported the differences.
	* Added several new test cases to handle new commands, updated system
	messages, and some test cases missed in prior releases.
	* Some code tidy up and style changes.

2007-01-28  Simon Baldwin <simon_baldwin@yahoo.com>

	* Release 1.3.6.

	* Removed "move" as a synonym for "go".  Having this extra command may
	confuse games that otherwise expect to override all movement.
	* Fixed a small object disambiguation bug.
	* Corrected a problem that occurred when listing objects that would not
	fit in a container when using multiple object targets.
	* Improved object disambiguation.  The parser should now allow, for
	example, "drop book" to cope with two books, one held but the other
	not.  Also, tightened "all" object generators to ignore static objects
	moved to player inventory by events.  Thanks to Nick Rogers for the
	suggestions.
	* Changed (again) the way game tasks with failing restrictions and
	library commands interact.  It seems that Adrift treats "take" in a
	special way that doesn't always let a game override, and some games
	appear to require this.
	* Added "+percent+" as a '%' character entity.  This appears to be an
	undocumented Runner feature.
	* Fixed a tricky bug with complex task commands.  Yet again, thanks to
	Nick Rogers for the report and test case.
	* Minor tidy up and performance improvements.

2006-12-16  Simon Baldwin <simon_baldwin@yahoo.com>

	* Release 1.3.5.

	* Fixed a crash caused by using referenced text in task restrictions on
	the very first game turn (before any referenced text set).  Thanks to
	Mark J. Tilford for the diagnosis.
	* Fixed an embarrassing crash that showed up when entering a line that
	contained two or more spaces and no other text, and another caused by
	failure to trap division by zero adequately.
	* Updated to Zlib 1.2.3.
	* Reworked game task handling to match the current understanding of how
	the Adrift Runner selects between multiple tasks that match player
	input.  Thanks (yet again) to Mark J. Tilford for the analysis and
	reverse engineering work.
	* Fixed a text formatting problem that showed up when examining an NPC
	that is both wearing and holding objects.  Thanks to Richard Otter.
	* Changes to score notifications to match the Runner a little better,
	and generally behave more usefully.
	* Implemented Adrift's "count" (alias "num") command, for querying the
	status of weight and size limitations for carried objects.
	* Fixed NPC task restriction off-by-one error in the version 3.8 to
	version 4.0 game conversions done on parsing a TAF file, an object
	worn task restriction error, and a win/lose game endian problem.
	* Improved the way tasks are run when NPCs encounter objects and other
	NPCs on walks, trying for closer agreement with the Adrift runner,
	and a walk bug that made NPCs move more often than they should in
	roomgroup walks.  Thanks to Todd Watson for the report.
	* Fixed an alternate room description problem, an event timing problem,
	a task override failure, and a task action ordering issue.  Thanks to
	Nick Rogers for the reports.
	* Corrected the way static objects moved to hidden by events behave.
	* Fixed a status line display ordering bug that showed up on game end
	with non-windowing Glk libraries.
	* Removed the generally useless os_flush() function from the interface.
	* Fixed a parser bug that prevented optional "{...}" words and wildcards
	from working correctly if placed last on a list.
	* Added period to the existing comma to period for characters used to
	separate elements on a multi-command input line.  This improves the
	match with the Adrift Runner.
	* Added lower-level game saving and loading functions, to allow
	interpreters such as Gargoyle to save and load game states directly to
	files or callbacks.  Added "-r FILE" to glkscare to restore a saved
	game directly the interpreter starts up.
	* Added a built-in random number generator that will produce a stable
	and repeatable sequence on all platforms.  Useful for scripted game
	testing, it may be selected in place of the platform's rand().
	* Improved memory allocations in the parser for better performance, and
	generally reduced strlen() calls on constant strings throughout.
	* Improved the printfilter, adding paths without allocate-and-copy for
	strings that don't use tags or other HTML-like formatting.
	* Updated event restarting to correctly reproduce Adrift's handling of
	this situation in version 3.9 and 3.8 Runners (bug compatibility?),
	and added other general event handling compatibility fixes.
	* Altered expression handling to accurately reproduce Adrift's rounded
	division, and also its apparently sequential and/or comparison.
	* Assorted refactoring and code format and style cleanups, including an
	effort at NPC accessors and mutators, removal of int16 types,
	rationalization of use of unsigned variables, and byte instead of
	short for boolean types.
	* Made code 64-bit clean (specifically, LP64, but probably also ILP64).
	* Added a control to allow the player to override the count of turns
	consumed by 'wait'.  This helps with games that set one value but
	assume another.  Several Runner releases are broken and always add one
	to turns on 'wait', no matter what the game requests.
	* Increased undo from a single turn to 17 total.  Added a command
	history, addressable with '!' (somewhat like the c-shell).
	* Improvements to 'it', 'him', and 'her' handling to try to better match
	the Adrift Runner.  Also, added an attempt at guessing whether objects
	are plural or singular, for correct "...is" and "...are" messages.
	Thanks to David Whyld for the report and test case.
	* Improved the parser, adding "...object {and object...}" and "...all
	except object {and object}..." to take, drop, and wear and remove.
	Rationalized assorted other commands, making "everything" synonymous
	with "all" and "apart from" synonymous with "[except/but] {for}".
	* Added a parser extension to logically interpolate %...% variables
	into task command patterns before matching them to user input.
	* Fixed getdynfromroom() for task command functions so that it works
	believably with 'Humbug'.
	* Added an automated test program, and a suite of test scripts aimed at
	verifying core interpreter features and regression testing games by
	comparing against transcripts created by the genuine Runner.

